# tests/test_session_age.bd - T25: SESSION AGE.
# Does one fixed workload run slower the longer the game has been open,
# and does restarting the game put it back?
#
# =====================================================================
# ONE QUESTION
# =====================================================================
#   Run ONE byte-identical, fixed, deterministic workload in three
#   session states:
#     A  immediately after a fresh game launch,
#     B  after 15+ minutes of ordinary play including two or three
#        menu.bd overload kills,
#     C  immediately after quitting and relaunching again,
#   and ask two things: is the work measurably slower in B, and does C
#   put it back?
#   THE MANIPULATION IS ENTIRELY OUTSIDE THIS FILE. Nothing in this
#   script changes between blocks - not one character. The only variable
#   is how old the session is when it runs.
#
# =====================================================================
# WHY THE RESTART BLOCK IS THE WHOLE POINT
# =====================================================================
#   "Later is slower" on its own is worth very little. A dozen things
#   drift one way across twenty minutes and none of them are separable.
#   "Later is slower AND a restart puts it back" is a single reversible
#   manipulation applied twice in opposite directions, which is as close
#   to a controlled experiment as this environment allows.
#   Block C is therefore not a nice-to-have. A rise in B WITHOUT a fall
#   in C does not confirm this hypothesis - it is a different and much
#   bigger finding (see WHAT EACH RESULT MEANS, outcome 2).
#
# =====================================================================
# WHY A NULL IS WORTH JUST AS MUCH
# =====================================================================
#   The five crashes that define the central mystery (+40.7s, +111.0s,
#   +40.3s, +65.5s, +64.3s) were ALL collected late in one long, heavily
#   crashed session, and have been treated ever since as five independent
#   samples of one distribution. If session age moves the number at all,
#   they are not independent and that whole block has to be re-collected.
#   If it does not, "no session carryover" stops being a player assertion
#   (field_notes ~line 644, ~line 822, and the bug report's own text) and
#   becomes a measurement at session scale - which leaves the per-run
#   phase effects as the only surviving explanation for that spread.
#
# =====================================================================
# THE FOUR MODELS THIS DISCRIMINATES
# =====================================================================
#   S1 NO SESSION EFFECT ..... the engine's throughput does not care how
#                              long it has been running. work= medians for
#                              A, B and C all land inside the run-to-run
#                              noise, every LINE1_cpu reads ~0.
#   S2 REVERSIBLE AGEING ..... something accumulates while the session is
#                              open (leaked process records, an unbounded
#                              event list, a decaying-average accumulator
#                              that never fully drains) and a relaunch
#                              clears it. work= B > A, work= C back to A.
#                              THE HYPOTHESIS UNDER TEST.
#   S3 DURABLE AGEING ........ B is slower and C stays slow. Whatever it
#                              is survives a process restart, so it lives
#                              in the SAVE, not in memory. Bigger finding
#                              than S2 and a different investigation.
#   S4 PER-RUN, NOT PER-SESSION  the four runs inside block B trend upward
#                              among THEMSELVES by about as much as B
#                              exceeds A. Then the accumulation is per-RUN
#                              (T20's territory, at a longer timescale),
#                              not a fixed session-age penalty, and the
#                              fix is forced idle between runs, not
#                              restarts.
#   S4 is why all four runs of every block get reported individually and
#   never collapsed to a median only.
#
# =====================================================================
# WHAT EACH RESULT MEANS
# =====================================================================
#   1. median work(B) > median work(A) + R, and median work(C) within
#      R/3 of median work(A)
#        -> S2 CONFIRMED. Session age is a real variance source, the
#           5-run variance block is confounded, every prior back-to-back
#           block in this investigation needs its session age recorded,
#           and the cheap mitigation ("restart before a measurement run")
#           is immediately actionable.
#   2. median work(B) > median work(A) + R, and C stays high
#        -> S3. Report this SEPARATELY and loudly. Something persisted
#           across a full relaunch, which no model in this programme
#           currently contains.
#   3. All three medians inside R/3 of each other, all LINE1_cpu ~0
#        -> S1 REFUTED-BY-NULL, and a useful null: the 5-run variance
#           block's samples were genuinely independent.
#   4. Block B's own four runs climb monotonically, by a margin
#      comparable to B-minus-A
#        -> S4. Re-run block B with 60s of forced idle between runs; if
#           the climb vanishes, it was per-run residue, not session age.
#   5. LINE1_cpu reads 0 on the very first script of a fresh boot (A1/C1)
#        -> settles an untested assumption nobody has separated: the load
#           meter is clean at game load, not merely clean because the
#           player idled before running. A NONZERO A1/C1 reading means
#           the meter carries a launch cost or a file-length cost and
#           every "cpu=0 on line 1" note in the corpus needs re-reading.
#   6. LINE1_cpu reads nonzero in block B but zero in A and C
#        -> direct meter-level evidence of session residue, independent
#           of the throughput measurement entirely. This is the cheapest
#           possible confirmation and it costs one call.
#
#   R = the run-to-run noise floor. Take it as the LARGER of:
#       (a) this test's own block-A range: max work= minus min work=
#           across FRESH 1-4, and
#       (b) T03's measured range for its fixed workload.
#       Using the larger is deliberate: it makes confirmation harder,
#       never easier, and it means this test still has a usable threshold
#       if T03 has not been run yet.
#
# =====================================================================
# OUTPUT FORMAT
# =====================================================================
#   Header (printed before any timing starts):
#     T25[tag] LINE1_cpu=<c> t_abs=<t>
#       LINE1_cpu = get_cpu() on executable line 1. Nothing in this
#                   process has run ahead of it - not the param read, not
#                   the timestamp, not one log(). Whatever it says was put
#                   there by the environment.
#       t_abs     = raw get_unix_time(), absolute on purpose, so the gap
#                   between consecutive runs can be reconstructed from the
#                   paste instead of guessed.
#   Per round (6 of them, structurally identical, each preceded by the
#   same sleep(2)):
#     T25[tag] R<n> pre=<c> burst=<s> post=<c> w=<s> at=<s>
#       R<n>  = round number FIRST on the line, so a truncated last line
#               still says how far the run got.
#       pre   = get_cpu() immediately before the burst. Instrument-
#               liveness check and matched control series only - it is
#               not a clean drain curve.
#       burst = seconds for 20 unrolled `a = a + 1` lines. THE THROUGHPUT
#               READOUT. Deliberately the SAME 20-line burst T20 uses, so
#               burst= values are directly comparable across both tests.
#       post  = get_cpu() straight after the burst, taken after the
#               bracket closed so it cannot contaminate burst=. If post
#               is not clearly above pre, the meter is not responding and
#               the run is void.
#       w     = RUNNING SUM of burst seconds so far. This is the headline
#               scalar, carried on every line so a killed run still
#               yields a partial one.
#       at    = seconds since line 1.
#   Final line (its absence means the run was killed - itself a result):
#     T25[tag] DONE rounds=6 a=120 work=<s> LINE1_cpu=<c> total=<s>
#               end_cpu=<c> t_abs=<t>
#       work= is the number the three blocks are compared on.
#       a=120 is the integrity check: 6 rounds x 20 increments. ANY other
#       value means the bursts did not execute the statement count this
#       source shows and every burst= in that run is void.
#       LINE1_cpu is repeated here so a paste that loses its top still
#       carries the primary meter reading.
#
# =====================================================================
# HOW TO READ IT
# =====================================================================
#   1. work= is the primary comparison, NOT total=. total= is ~60%
#      sleep(), which is real time and constant, so a 10% throughput loss
#      shows up diluted there and undiluted in work=. Report both; trust
#      work=.
#   2. Compare MEDIANS of the four work= values per block, not means. One
#      outlier per block is expected and must not be allowed to decide
#      the result.
#   3. Then look INSIDE each block at R0..R5 and at run 1..4. A block that
#      is slower on average but flat within itself is a session-age
#      penalty; a block that climbs within itself is per-run accumulation
#      (S4).
#   4. Report the full float tail on every cpu value. 93.75 and
#      49.94140625 are dyadic rationals; 10.0000001798557 is not. Which
#      family a reading belongs to says whether it came out of the same
#      arithmetic as the in-run readings.
#   5. INTEGER CLOCK FALLBACK: if every burst= prints as a whole number,
#      get_unix_time() is 1-second granular (the official reference does
#      declare it "-> int"). Individual bursts are then useless, but
#      work= aggregates six of them and total= aggregates the whole run -
#      both survive the quantisation well enough to compare blocks. Say
#      so in the paste if you see it.
#
# =====================================================================
# HOW TO RUN - three blocks, one session-and-a-half. @param is POSITIONAL.
# =====================================================================
#   The tag is the only argument and it is free text: use the block
#   letter plus the run number, e.g.  run test_session_age A1
#
#   BLOCK A - FRESH BOOT (~2.5 min at the terminal):
#     1. Fully quit the game and relaunch it (or reload the save from
#        scratch). Do not run, connect to, or open anything first.
#     2. Make `run test_session_age A1` the VERY FIRST command of the
#        session. Nothing before it, not even `ps` - A1's LINE1_cpu is
#        the pristine "is the meter clean at game load" reading and any
#        command typed ahead of it spends that measurement.
#     3. NOW run `ps`, `jobs` and `daemons` and paste all three.
#     4. `run test_session_age A2`, then A3, then A4, back to back.
#     5. For ONE of them use `time run test_session_age A4` instead, so
#        the terminal's own clock brackets the run from outside. That is
#        the only instrument here that costs the measured script nothing.
#
#   BLOCK B - AGED SESSION (15+ min of play, then ~2.5 min at the
#   terminal):
#     6. Without restarting, play normally for at least 15 minutes: run
#        menu.bd two or three times and let it crash as usual, connect to
#        something, browse, whatever an ordinary session contains. Age it
#        REALISTICALLY - do not invent a special workload, that would
#        just be a different experiment.
#     7. Run `ps`, `jobs`, `daemons` and paste them.
#     8. THEN SIT IDLE AT THE PROMPT FOR 60 SECONDS. This is not
#        optional. It drains any short-timescale post-kill residue (T20's
#        subject, which decays in seconds) so that anything still visible
#        afterwards is session-scale. Note the wall-clock gap between the
#        last overload kill and B1.
#     9. `run test_session_age B1`, B2, B3, B4, back to back; `time` one
#        of them as in step 5.
#    10. Write down: how long the session had been open, roughly how many
#        scripts were run, and how many overload kills occurred.
#
#   BLOCK C - POST-RESTART (~2.5 min at the terminal):
#    11. Fully quit and relaunch again.
#    12. `run test_session_age C1` as the VERY FIRST command, exactly as
#        in step 2. Then `ps`/`jobs`/`daemons`, then C2, C3, C4, with one
#        `time`d run.
#
#   Paste EVERY line of EVERY run including any kill banner, labelled by
#   tag, plus the three command pastes per block and the block-B session
#   notes. A run that prints only its header is a valid result - paste it.
#
# =====================================================================
# SAFETY / IS THE BURST THE EXPERIMENT?
# =====================================================================
#   NO - the burst here is an INSTRUMENT, not the experiment, and this
#   script is built never to die. The longest contiguous unpaced stretch
#   is 27 executed lines (loop condition, 2 instrument reads, 20 burst
#   lines, the accumulator, the log and the counter) = ~2.7s of
#   continuous load at base tier, against a documented ~5s kill window.
#   The 11-line prologue is followed by its own sleep(2), so round 0 is
#   entered from exactly the same idle state as rounds 1-5 and every
#   round is structurally identical. If this script IS killed, that is a
#   finding about the block it was killed in, not a design failure -
#   report the tag and the last R line.
#
# RUNTIME: ~30s per run. 4 runs per block = ~2.5 min at the terminal per
#   block; 12 runs = ~6 min of script time total, spread across two game
#   restarts and 15+ minutes of ordinary play. No single sitting exceeds
#   ~2.5 minutes.
#
# REPEATS: 4 per block, 12 total. If A and B separate but C lands
#   ambiguously between them, add 4 more runs to C - the reversal is the
#   load-bearing claim and C is the block that carries it.
#
# @param tag "block+run label printed on every line, e.g. A1, B3, C2" default="?"

var c0 = get_cpu()
var t0 = get_unix_time()
var TAG = get_param("tag", "?")
log("T25[" + TAG + "] LINE1_cpu=" + str(c0) + " t_abs=" + str(t0))
var a = 0
var r = 0
var w = 0
var cb = 0
var ts = 0
var te = 0
sleep(2)

while r < 6:
    cb = get_cpu()
    ts = get_unix_time()
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    te = get_unix_time()
    w = w + (te - ts)
    log("T25[" + TAG + "] R" + str(r) + " pre=" + str(cb) + " burst=" + str(te - ts) + " post=" + str(get_cpu()) + " w=" + str(w) + " at=" + str(te - t0))
    r = r + 1
    sleep(2)

log("T25[" + TAG + "] DONE rounds=" + str(r) + " a=" + str(a) + " (a must be 120) work=" + str(w) + " LINE1_cpu=" + str(c0) + " total=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + " end_cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " t_abs=" + str(get_unix_time()))
